<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Phaistos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 150 BCE (approx.) · capital Phaistos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city-state built on the ruins of the Minoan palace in southern Crete, rival of Gortyn until destroyed by it c. 150 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace fell, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Phaistos</a> survived as a settlement on its ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesara-plain">Mesara</a> plain. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Dorian-speaking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> dominated it, and by the Archaic period it was an independent polis, one of many on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, with Greek temples built over parts of the old palace.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Phaistos</a> controlled the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesara-plain">Mesara</a> and the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matala">Matala</a>. It minted coins from the 4th century BCE showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talos">Talos</a>, the bronze giant of myth, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. Like other Cretan cities it had an aristocratic constitution with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a> magistrates and men's messes. Its great rival was <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gortyn">Gortyn</a>, a few kilometres east.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Phaistos</a> was destroyed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gortyn">Gortyn</a> in the mid-2nd century BCE and its territory absorbed. The site was later known chiefly for its Minoan palace, described separately as the Palace State of Phaistos.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/phaistos.jpg" alt="Phaistos" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: User:Bibi Saint-Pol · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Minoan_Crete-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phaistos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Phaestus, Classical Phaistos</dd></dl></aside></div>

